    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So it's more about describing the *type* of mystery rather than trying to force a name into the field. That's helpful. I guess my main concern is how to present this to the end-users. If a researcher sees \"Obscured Origin\" or \"Cryptic Markings,\" they might think the data is unreliable or that the archive is just incomplete. Is there a way to frame these \"unidentifiable\" elements as a legitimate part of the object's historical profile rather than just a gap in our knowledge?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are wise to consider the user experience; you don't want your metadata to look like a patch job, but rather like an artifact itself. To frame these ambiguities as legitimate historical features, you should leverage the `dc:rights` or `dc:coverage` elements, or even add a dedicated `dc:relation` pointing to an internal notes/provenance record. In the description, frame the uncertainty as a *feature* of the map's history—for instance, \"Map exhibits characteristics of clandestine cartography, suggesting deliberate obfuscation of origin.\" This shifts the narrative from \"we don't know\" to \"this object *is* a mystery.\"",
      "source": "target",
